.Dd October 28, 2026
.Dt XXD 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm xxd
.Nd make a hex dump, or turn one back into bytes
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl a
.Op Fl b | e | i | p
.Op Fl C
.Op Fl c Ar cols
.Op Fl d
.Op Fl g Ar bytes
.Op Fl l Ar len
.Op Fl n Ar name
.Op Fl o Ar off
.Op Fl s Oo + Oc Ns Oo - Oc Ns Ar seek
.Op Fl u
.Op Ar infile Op Ar outfile
.Nm
.Fl r
.Op Fl p
.Op Fl c Ar cols
.Op Fl s Oo - Oc Ns Ar off
.Op Ar infile Op Ar outfile
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
writes a hex dump of
.Ar infile ,
or of the standard input when it is missing or
.Sq - ,
to
.Ar outfile
or the standard output.
Each line holds the offset, the bytes in hexadecimal in groups, and the
same bytes as text, with a dot for each byte that does not print:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
00000000: 6865 6c6c 6f0a                           hello.
.Ed
.Pp
With
.Fl r
it reads such a dump, or a plain one, and writes the bytes back.
.Pp
Numbers may be given in decimal, in hexadecimal with
.Ql 0x ,
or in octal with a leading
.Ql 0 .
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl a
Write a single
.Ql *
in place of consecutive lines of zero bytes.
.It Fl b
Show the bytes in binary, six to a line, instead of hexadecimal.
.It Fl C
With
.Fl i ,
write the variable names in capitals.
.It Fl c Ar cols
Put
.Ar cols
bytes on each line: 16 by default, 30 with
.Fl p ,
12 with
.Fl i
and 6 with
.Fl b ;
at most 256.
.It Fl d
Show the offsets in decimal.
.It Fl E
Not supported: EBCDIC.
.It Fl e
Little-endian dump: the bytes of each group are shown in reverse order,
four to a group by default.
.It Fl g Ar bytes
Put
.Ar bytes
bytes in each group: 2 by default, 1 with
.Fl b ,
4 with
.Fl e .
With 0, a line is one group.
.It Fl h
Print a summary of the options.
.It Fl i
Write C source: an
.Vt unsigned char
array of the bytes, named after
.Ar infile ,
and an
.Vt unsigned int
of its length.
From the standard input, only the bytes.
.It Fl l Ar len
Stop after
.Ar len
bytes.
.It Fl n Ar name
With
.Fl i ,
name the variables after
.Ar name
instead of the file.
.It Fl o Ar off
Add
.Ar off
to the offsets shown.
.It Fl p
Plain dump: hexadecimal digits only, 30 bytes to a line.
.It Fl R Ar when
Colour; accepted for compatibility, never used.
.It Fl r
Turn a dump back into bytes.
Each line's offset says where its bytes go;
.Ar outfile ,
if it exists, is patched in place rather than truncated, and gaps are
filled with zeros where it cannot seek.
With
.Fl p ,
the input is read as plain hexadecimal digits, and anything else is
ignored.
.It Fl s Ar seek
Start at
.Ar seek
bytes into
.Ar infile ;
with a minus sign, that many bytes before its end; with
.Ql + ,
past the standard input's current position.
With
.Fl r ,
add
.Ar seek
to the offsets read.
.It Fl u
Use capital hexadecimal letters.
.It Fl v
Print the version.
.El
.Pp
The long spellings
.Fl cols ,
.Fl groupsize ,
.Fl len ,
.Fl seek ,
.Fl ps ,
.Fl plain ,
.Fl include ,
.Fl bits ,
.Fl revert ,
.Fl version
and
.Fl help
are accepted too.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 for a bad option or argument, 2 when
.Ar infile
cannot be read, 3 when
.Ar outfile
cannot be written and 4 when the seek fails.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Dump the first 32 bytes of a file:
.Dl xxd -l 32 file
.Pp
Change byte 0x10 of a file to 0xff:
.Dl echo 10: ff | xxd -r - file
.Pp
Copy a file through a plain dump:
.Dl xxd -p file | xxd -r -p > copy
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr hexdump 1 ,
.Xr od 1
.Sh HISTORY
Minkar's
.Nm
is its own, written to behave as the one on macOS.
